WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.836
This chapter covers four breathing complaints and patterns the final asks about.

2
00:00:06.836 --> 00:00:16.418
First orthopnea, trouble breathing when lying down, along with its relatives, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea and platypnea.

3
00:00:16.418 --> 00:00:25.050
Then three abnormal breathing patterns: Kussmaul breathing, Cheyne-Stokes respiration and Biot's respiration.

4
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:31.148
For each one you need what it looks like, what causes it, and why it happens.

5
00:00:31.148 --> 00:00:36.375
The patterns are easy to mix up, so we will put them side by side.

6
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:49.962
RCP 203 defines orthopnea as a patient's sensation of uncomfortable breathing when lying down, typically relieved by sitting or standing up.

7
00:00:49.962 --> 00:01:04.643
Like dyspnea, it goes with many cardiac and pulmonary disorders, but it is most commonly linked to fluid overload and cardiac conditions such as CHF and pulmonary edema.

8
00:01:04.643 --> 00:01:11.071
Lying flat increases venous return and redistributes fluid into the lungs.

9
00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:27.750
Hess explains it in chapter forty: when the patient is supine, less blood pools in the legs, so blood shifts into the central circulation, filling pressures rise, and interstitial edema forms.

10
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:43.431
Hess lists other causes of orthopnea too: severe COPD, from hyperinflation without necessarily worse heart function, sleep apnea, and other chronic respiratory diseases.

11
00:01:43.431 --> 00:01:55.078
At the bedside, ask how many pillows the patient sleeps on, because orthopnea may be hidden in someone who already sleeps on two or three.

12
00:01:55.078 --> 00:02:09.680
Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea is an air hunger or drowning feeling that wakes the patient one to two hours after falling asleep, and it commonly suggests cardiac dysfunction.

13
00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:20.399
RCP 103 says after several hours of sleep, and Hess says several hours after lying down, but use one to two hours on this exam.

14
00:02:20.399 --> 00:02:27.067
Platypnea is the opposite of orthopnea: difficulty breathing unless lying flat.

15
00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:34.697
Kussmaul breathing is deep and fast, labored, and regular, with no apnea.

16
00:02:34.697 --> 00:02:37.173
The patient looks air hungry.

17
00:02:37.173 --> 00:02:51.341
Its classic cause is diabetic ketoacidosis, or any severe metabolic acidosis such as renal failure, and it is compensatory: the patient is blowing off carbon dioxide.

18
00:02:51.341 --> 00:02:55.694
Cheyne-Stokes breathing waxes and wanes with apnea.

19
00:02:55.694 --> 00:03:06.960
Breaths get gradually deeper and faster, then shallower, then a brief apnea, and the cycle repeats, a crescendo decrescendo pattern.

20
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:15.495
Its causes are CHF, stroke, brain injury with raised intracranial pressure, and central sleep apnea.

21
00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:23.604
Biot's respiration is irregular breaths with equal tidal volumes, followed by periods of apnea.

22
00:03:23.604 --> 00:03:34.017
Its causes are central nervous system injury, raised intracranial pressure and brainstem damage, and Hess adds meningitis.

23
00:03:35.433 --> 00:03:43.255
Biot's and Cheyne-Stokes both include apnea, so separate them by the size of the breaths.

24
00:03:43.255 --> 00:03:52.571
Hess describes Biot's as a short burst of uniform, deep breaths, then apnea lasting ten to thirty seconds.

25
00:03:52.571 --> 00:03:58.812
Biot's breaths are equal in size; Cheyne-Stokes breaths build and fade.

26
00:03:58.812 --> 00:04:05.667
Biot's points to brainstem involvement and more severe neurologic dysfunction.

27
00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:08.831
One conflict to know about Kussmaul.

28
00:04:08.831 --> 00:04:21.047
The RCP 103 deck describes it as deep and fast, but Hess chapter four describes it as deep and labored, with a normal or reduced frequency.

29
00:04:21.047 --> 00:04:26.408
Use the deck, and Hess chapter fifty two also calls it rapid.

30
00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:29.765
Pause and work this out.

31
00:04:29.765 --> 00:04:40.952
A patient with a head injury breathes in short bursts of uniform, deep breaths, each burst followed by apnea lasting ten to thirty seconds.

32
00:04:40.952 --> 00:04:46.183
Which pattern is this, and what does it suggest about the injury?

33
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:53.100
[6-second practice pause]

34
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.712
This is Biot's respiration.

35
00:04:55.712 --> 00:05:05.175
The breaths in each burst are equal in size, which separates it from Cheyne-Stokes, where breaths build up and fade away.

36
00:05:05.175 --> 00:05:21.600
Biot's points to brainstem involvement and more severe neurologic dysfunction, and its causes are central nervous system injury, raised intracranial pressure and brainstem damage, with meningitis added by Hess.

37
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:29.547
Kussmaul breathing is the body's compensation for a metabolic acidosis.

38
00:05:29.547 --> 00:05:37.015
Hyperventilating expels carbon dioxide, which is an acid when dissolved in blood.

39
00:05:37.015 --> 00:05:44.207
The RCP 103 diabetic ketoacidosis case shows how far that compensation can go.

40
00:05:44.207 --> 00:05:55.180
The patient is breathing deep and fast, Kussmaul respiration, at twenty four breaths per minute, with a very low PaCO2.

41
00:05:55.180 --> 00:06:00.436
Yet the pH stays critically low, at six point nine eight.

42
00:06:00.436 --> 00:06:03.755
So the compensation is only partial.

43
00:06:03.755 --> 00:06:10.025
The breathing is helping, but it cannot fully correct the acid load.

44
00:06:11.433 --> 00:06:21.155
Cheyne-Stokes breathing comes from delayed communication between the lungs and the brain about carbon dioxide.

45
00:06:21.155 --> 00:06:33.794
With a long circulation time, as in CHF and low cardiac output, the brain responds to carbon dioxide changes too late and keeps overcorrecting.

46
00:06:33.794 --> 00:06:42.191
Hess chapter forty five adds that apnea starts when the PaCO2 drops below the apneic threshold.

47
00:06:42.191 --> 00:06:53.769
Cheyne-Stokes is seen in twenty five to forty percent of CHF patients, and treatment is aimed mainly at improving cardiac function.

48
00:06:53.769 --> 00:07:03.579
The RCP 103 sleep apnea deck adds that adaptive servo ventilation, ASV, helps this type of central sleep apnea.

49
00:07:03.579 --> 00:07:14.008
Biot's comes from impaired central respiratory control: brainstem injury, raised intracranial pressure, or meningitis.

50
00:07:15.433 --> 00:07:16.169
One more.

51
00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:27.128
A patient with heart failure and low cardiac output has breaths that build up and then fade, followed by a brief apnea, over and over.

52
00:07:27.128 --> 00:07:33.833
Name the pattern, explain why it happens, and say where treatment is mainly aimed.

53
00:07:34.733 --> 00:07:40.733
[6-second practice pause]

54
00:07:41.233 --> 00:07:43.953
This is Cheyne-Stokes breathing.

55
00:07:43.953 --> 00:07:57.634
With low cardiac output, blood takes longer to carry carbon dioxide changes from the lungs to the brain, so the brain responds too late and keeps overcorrecting.

56
00:07:57.634 --> 00:08:03.242
When the PaCO2 falls below the apneic threshold, the apnea begins.

57
00:08:03.242 --> 00:08:16.583
Because the root problem is the slow circulation, treatment is aimed mainly at improving cardiac function, and ASV can help this type of central sleep apnea.

58
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.487
Here is the chapter in brief.

59
00:08:20.487 --> 00:08:32.579
Orthopnea is uncomfortable breathing lying down, most often from heart failure, because lying flat shifts blood into the central circulation.

60
00:08:32.579 --> 00:08:34.123
Ask about pillows.

61
00:08:34.123 --> 00:08:45.186
Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea wakes the patient one to two hours after falling asleep, and platypnea is the opposite of orthopnea.

62
00:08:45.186 --> 00:08:54.105
Kussmaul is deep, fast and regular, compensating for a metabolic acidosis such as diabetic ketoacidosis.

63
00:08:54.105 --> 00:09:03.796
Cheyne-Stokes builds and fades with apnea, from a delayed carbon dioxide signal in heart failure or brain injury.

64
00:09:03.796 --> 00:09:10.400
Biot's is equal bursts of breaths with apnea, and it points to the brainstem.
